<!--
  ============================================================================
  JawaGroundHulk.xml                     Jawa_Patches · authored 2026-08-13
  ============================================================================

  V1 ROW 4, RIDER: the GROUND HULK. The PrefabDef half.

  WHAT THE PLAYER SEES: the rest of the wreck. The gravship is the part the
  clan got flying; this is the part that stayed on the ground. A bow section
  of gravship deck lying in the sand — broken substructure and intact
  substructure in patches, a torn bite cut through the starboard flank, and
  thirty-one ancient cryptosleep caskets still standing in their rows inside
  the hold. The Hutts cut in first and took the cargo. The Jawas are the
  SECOND scavengers on this wreck, and the caskets are what the first crew
  did not think was worth the trouble.

  ===========================================================================
  THE FOOTPRINT IS NOT INVENTED. IT IS THE SHIP.
  ===========================================================================
  Stamped from BRIDGE's own gravship export, not from a shape generator:

    C:\Users\Mandrake\AppData\LocalLow\Ludeon Studios\RimWorld by Ludeon Studios\Config\GravshipExport\Gravship.xml

  That file is a ShipLayoutDefV2: 135 rows of 88, 4,057 occupied cells. In
  that format the OUTER <li> is z running SOUTH to NORTH and the INNER <li>
  is x running WEST to EAST, so rows[0][0] is the SOUTH-WEST corner. Read
  that way and printed north-up, the ship is a broad forward delta on a
  spine with two swept wings and a twin-rail tail.

  The crop taken here is the BOW: source rows z=118..133, x=9..81 — 691
  occupied cells in a 73 x 16 box. It was chosen because z=123..130 is the
  only band of the whole ship that is a SINGLE unbroken run 51 to 55 cells
  wide. That is a hold. Everything south of it splits into three separate
  runs (port wing, spine, starboard wing) and everything north of it frays
  into the prow tips, so this is the one slice of the ship that reads as a
  room rather than as a slice.

  ⚠ CROP CEILING WAS 1,200 CELLS AND THIS IS 619 STAMPED. Deliberately
  under. The ceiling is a ceiling; 691 raw cells minus 76 failure gaps plus
  4 cells of plating thrown clear of the breach is what the bay actually is.
  Widening the crop south to z=115 would buy ~130 more cells and would buy
  them as two disconnected 20-wide wing stubs, which is the "random slice"
  the ruling told us not to ship.

  ===========================================================================
  TERRAIN — EVERY defName READ OFF DISK
  ===========================================================================
  BrokenSubstructure   Gravship Crashes, packageId Arcjc007.GravshipCrashes
                       C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\workshop\content\294100\3578515873\1.6\Defs\Terrain\Terrain_Foundation.xml:4-35
                       ParentName FloorBase, isFoundation true, pathCost is
                       not set so it inherits 0, terrainAffordanceNeeded
                       Walkable, no placeWorkers. It sits on ordinary desert.

  Substructure         Odyssey, C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\RimWorld\Data\Odyssey\Defs\TerrainDefs\Terrain_Foundation.xml:4-36
                       Same shape, same isFoundation true, same affordance
                       list — which is why the two tile together cleanly.

  🔴 NOT A SOLID SLAB, AND THAT IS THE WHOLE POINT.
  The BrokenSubstructure motif is large and high-contrast; laid edge to edge
  it tiles into wallpaper at any zoom the player actually uses. So the deck
  is INTERLEAVED: 278 cells broken, 341 cells intact, 76 cells left as bare
  desert. 45% broken / 55% intact, assigned from smoothed value noise on a
  5-cell lattice (fixed seed) so the two form irregular patches of three to
  six cells rather than a checkerboard, and so no two parts of the deck
  repeat. A real wreck fails in patches, so the failure rate is not uniform
  either: it rises with how frayed the hull outline already is at that cell,
  and it goes to certainty inside the breach.

  ===========================================================================
  THE BREACH
  ===========================================================================
  An elliptical bite centred at local (58,7), radii 9 x 5, cut clean through
  the STARBOARD flank of the hold and opening east. Cells inside it are gone
  entirely; cells on its rim are eroded but not removed; four cells of
  BrokenSubstructure lie OUTSIDE the hull line beyond the mouth, which is
  plating thrown clear. The starboard casket bank is deliberately short
  three units where the cut passes through it — that is the cargo the Hutts
  took, and the empty deck where it stood is the only evidence left.

  <rotations>North</rotations> locks the stamp, so the breach always opens
  EAST. See the honest limitation noted in the GenStepDef file.

  ===========================================================================
  PROPS — CHECKED AGAINST THE SALVAGE PALETTE, NOT GUESSED
  ===========================================================================
  AncientCryptosleepCasket   Core, C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\RimWorld\Data\Core\Defs\ThingDefs_Buildings\Buildings_Misc.xml:1084-1130
                             size (1,2), thingClass Building_AncientCryptosleepCasket,
                             no designationCategory, so spawn-only.
                             design/Jawa/art/salvage_palette.tsv:826 — USABLE-YIELDS,
                             deconstructible true, fraction 0.5.

  🔴 NEVER AncientCryptosleepPod. Core Buildings_Ancient_Active.xml:5.
     salvage_palette.tsv:23 lists it EXCLUDED — deconstructible FALSE. It is
     one word away from the right def and it is permanently unstrippable,
     which would break the ruling that this wreck is strippable. The word to
     check for is CASKET.

  ShipChunk_Mech             Odyssey, C:\Program Files (x86)\Steam\steamapps\common\RimWorld\Data\Odyssey\Defs\ThingDefs_Buildings\Buildings_Gravship.xml:65-89
                             size (2,2), Graphic_Random, rotatable false,
                             terrainAffordanceNeeded Heavy.
                             salvage_palette.tsv:870 — USABLE-YIELDS at
                             deconstruct fraction 1.0, Steel 40 + GravlitePanel 15.
                             It is literally a chunk of ship, on a ship.
                             Graphic_Random means six of them do not read as
                             copy-paste.

  LAYOUT RULE FOLLOWED: at play zoom a 1x1 prop is about 22px and a lone one
  is a speck nobody registers, so nothing here is placed singly. The caskets
  are in three banks of touching rows running across the hold, and the six
  ship chunks sit among and between those banks to break the grid.

  ⚠ THE CASKETS ARE SHUT AND STAY SHUT. Vanilla sleepers can wake hostile;
  that is a feature for later, not for v1. v1 needs only the image.

  ⚠ TUNING, AND IT IS THE ONE NUMBER WORTH ARGUING ABOUT: 31 caskets is
  31 x (90 steel + 2.5 uranium) = 2,790 steel and 77 uranium if every one is
  deconstructed, plus 240 steel and 90 gravlite panel from the chunks. That
  is roughly twice what the whole scrapfields scatter yields per map. It is
  one-off, non-renewing and gated behind deconstruct work on a wreck the
  player has to walk to, so it does not create a ladder (concept.md §3), but
  it IS an economy nudge nobody specified. It is the FIRST number to cut.
  Also note each casket carries CompProperties_Explosive, radius 2.66 Flame:
  destroying one in a touching row can chain. Deconstructing does not.

  ===========================================================================
  SAFETY
  ===========================================================================
  MayRequire names BOTH mods this def cannot exist without. If either is
  absent the whole def is dropped at load and nothing dangles — which is
  also why Patches/JawaGroundHulk_Register.xml gates its genStep
  registration on the SAME condition. Do not change one without the other.

  ⚠ ONE MECHANISM HERE IS NOT VERIFIED OFFLINE, stated so nobody reads this
  as proven: BrokenSubstructure is a FOUNDATION terrain (isFoundation true),
  and Gravship Crashes places it with map.terrainGrid.SetFoundation — see
  its own source at ...\3578515873\1.6\Source\GravshipCrashes\Util\GravshipSpawnUtility.cs:581.
  Whether PrefabDef terrain application routes an isFoundation terrain to
  the foundation layer or writes it to the top layer could not be read off
  disk (no decompiler on this machine). Both outcomes render, because both
  layers draw, and the broken/intact interleave reads either way — so the
  ART is safe under both. What is NOT certain under the top-layer outcome is
  prop placement, because a top-layer Substructure exposes only the
  Substructure affordance and ShipChunk_Mech needs Heavy. If the caskets or
  chunks come up missing on the first load, that is the cause and the fix is
  a one-line terrain swap, not a redesign.
  ============================================================================
-->
